      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "$$\nA=N,\\,B={7,6,5,4,…}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:",
      "worked_solution": "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-1 \\\\  & =6-7 \\\\  & =5-6 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =7+(n-1)(-1) \\\\  & =7-n+1 \\\\  & =8-n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB={8-n∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $7$ | $6$ | $5$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $8-n$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=8-n\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
